  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 PM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n- Heat Through This Evening, Some Storms Overnight\n\nSurface dew points have overachieved this afternoon compared to most \nmodel guidance, and as a result heat indices have nudged higher. \nWidespread indices of 95 to 100 are occurring and will continue to \noccur across southern Lower Michigan through this afternoon and \nevening given air temps in the upper 80s to low 90s and dew points \nof 70 to 75 making it feel pretty miserable in the sun. Reduce \nstrenuous outdoor activities through the evening.\n\nIn spite of favorable MLCAPE values of 1500-2500 J/kg this afternoon \nas well as 0-6 km shear values of 40-50 kts, some MLCIN of 100-200 \nJ/kg is still present north of I-96 where some convection has \noccurred but has largely been held in check. A LLJ of 30-35 kts is \npresent overhead our region, though it is more convergent over SE \nLower Michigan toward the Thumb region. That is where scattered \nstrong to severe storms are most likely to develop into this \nevening. Much of our region will be storm free through early tonight \nwith the exception of north of M-20 and near/east of US 127. \n\nBeyond midnight tonight, the LLJ over Wisconsin increases back to 30-\n35 kts and angles over Lake Michigan. Scattered showers/storms may \ndevelop especially from Muskegon to Lansing during the 05z-12z time \nframe. Most are not likely to reach severe levels. Model soundings \ndo support good CAPE through the hail growth region but an inversion \naround 850 mb will likely (though not with 100% certainty) limit \ndamaging wind potential. Localized downpours may lead to a few minor \nlowland and/or street flooding occurrences overnight.\n\n- Periodic Storms Thursday Afternoon into Late Night\n\nThere are some mixed signals regarding Thursday's daytime storm \nchances, leading to less confidence than desired (eg. NBM POPs are \naround 30%). Deep layer shear of around 40 kts will be present north \nof I-96. HREF mean SBCAPE values are less substantial than today but \nstill 1000-2000 J/kg. We do have upper jet divergence moving through \nwhich could aid in the development of some organized convection \nduring the midday to afternoon period. The low level jet weakens to \n20-25 kts. There is an incoherent signal amongst HREF members as to \nthe placement and intensity of convection tomorrow. The HRRR/RRFS do \nbring in some organized swaths of storms with 60+ mph gusts during \nthe afternoon, while the other CAMs are not supportive. At this \npoint, the SPC Marginal Risk seems to cover the spatial threat well, \nwith some isolated/scattered instances of strong wind gusts and hail \npossible.\n\nAfter 00z, a stronger nocturnal LLJ develops to our west and angles \nin during the late night hours. Very high PWAT values are shown by \nthe 12z ECMWF during this time, with 2.00-2.50\" moving in. Very \nheavy downpours and some street flooding may develop if organized \nconvection gets going on the nose of the LLJ Thursday night into \nFriday morning. Large hail also looks to be a threat with these \npotential storms.\n\n- Short Break Midday Friday, Then More Rain Chances\n\nA decent model signal exists for a break in the rain chances late\nFriday morning into the early afternoon period. Daytime heating on\nFriday could bring some pop-up showers and thunderstorms by\nevening with perhaps additional rain chances overnight along a\npotential surface boundary in the area.",
